UNIT schema_lint
KIND FUNCTIONAL
FIDELITY package
ANCHOR rederive@6199e2cc8b75 cli/sir/v05.mjs schemaLint over cli/sir/sir-parse.mjs parse (legacy mode) — lint 4 of the v0.5 pack gate; reproduce it exactly, accidents included
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG schema_lint(text: text, carried_name: text | none) -> record[hits: list[record[line: u64, what: text, text: text]]]
ENVELOPE text is any well-formed text (line breaks of every kind, any Unicode). carried_name is none or any well-formed text,
  the empty text included; the only caller passes none or the identifier of the primary's CARRIED line, but the rule below
  reads every name literally, so no name is OUT. Inside the envelope nothing throws.
DEPENDS-ON none
DESCRIPTION The pack gate's lint against carried data smuggled under a dependency declaration. The file is read the way the
  SIR parser reads it (R1 to R8): directives, their arguments, continuation lines, attribute values and comments. The lint
  OVER-reads: every DEPENDS-ON directive whose CONTENT (R9) — all of those parts, quoted text included — mentions carried
  data, either by the word carried or by carried_name as a whole word, is reported. The result is a record with one field,
  hits: the reported directives in file order, each a record of line (the directive's 1-based line number), what (the WHAT
  pin) and text (R11).
BEHAVIOR
R1 LINES: text is split at every CRLF pair, every other CR and every other LF; n breaks give n + 1 lines, numbered from 1.
  On each line, every U+FEFF inside the line's leading run of SP and U+FEFF characters (in any mix) is removed, then the
  trailing run of SP characters is removed. "The line" below always means this trimmed line. CITED FIDELITY package
R2 BLANK: an empty line is skipped. It ends nothing: lines after it still belong to the directive before it.
R3 COMMENT LINE: a line whose first character after its leading SP run is "#" opens nothing and ends nothing. Its COMMENT
  TEXT is that part with its leading run of "#" removed and then at most one SP character removed; it is appended to the
  comments of the current directive (the last one opened), or ignored before the first directive.
R4 CODE: on any other line, take the part after the leading SP run and cut it at its COMMENT MARKER: the first "#" that is
  preceded by an SP character, is followed by an SP character or the end, and is not inside a quoted span (R8) of that part.
  The code is the part before the marker with its trailing SP run removed, or the whole part when there is no marker; the
  text after the marker with its leading and trailing SP runs removed (possibly empty) is a TRAILING COMMENT, appended to the
  comments of the directive the line opens or continues (R5, R6).
R5 DIRECTIVE LINE: a line with no leading SP character opens a new directive when its NAME — its code up to the first SP
  character, or all of it — is a directive name, i.e. an uppercase ASCII letter followed by any number of uppercase ASCII
  letters, ASCII digits and "-", other than the word CITED. A name that is such a word followed by one ":" counts as that
  word (except CITED followed by ":"). The opened directive's ARGUMENTS are the attribute body (R7) of the code after the
  name, and that code's attribute values begin its attribute list; the directive ends where the next directive opens. It is
  a DEPENDS-ON directive when the name is exactly DEPENDS-ON (case-sensitive; so DEPENDS-ON: also is, while DEPENDS-ON-X,
  DEPENDS-ONX and depends-on are other directives). CITED FIDELITY package
R6 CONTINUATION: every other line — an indented line, or a column-0 line that does not open a directive (prose, a
  lowercase word, a name glued to its text by a character that is not SP, a CITED line) — belongs to the current directive:
  the attribute body (R7) of its code, when not empty, is appended to that directive's continuations, and its attribute
  values to its attribute list. A line before the first directive belongs to nothing and is ignored, so an indented
  DEPENDS-ON line is never a directive.
R7 ATTRIBUTES of a piece of code: read the piece with one space put in front. An ATTRIBUTE MARKER is a run of SP
  characters, then one of the words CITED, EARNED or ANCHOR (case-sensitive), then an SP character or the end, where the
  word does not lie inside a quoted span (R8) of the piece. The BODY is everything before the first marker's SP run (the
  whole piece when there is none), with its leading and trailing SP runs removed. Each marker's VALUE is the text after its
  word up to the next marker's SP run (or the end), with its leading and trailing SP runs removed — possibly empty, and kept
  even when empty. The marker itself (its SP run and its word) is in no part.
R8 QUOTED SPAN: scan left to right. A '"' opens a span when it is the first character, or the character before it is one
  of the OPENERS, or it directly follows the closing quote of the span before it. The span closes at the next '"' that is
  not escaped, where a backslash escapes the one character (code point) after it. An opening quote with no closing quote
  opens nothing, and no later quote opens either. A position is inside a span when it lies strictly between its two quotes.
  Spans only hide a "#" or an attribute word from R4 and R7; their text is content like any other. CITED FIDELITY package —
  so an inch mark (12"), a quote after a letter, after "/" or "." or after * + ? opens nothing
R9 CONTENT of a DEPENDS-ON directive: its arguments (possibly empty), then each of its continuations in line order, then each
  value of its attribute list in order (the directive line's first, left to right), then each of its comments in line order,
  joined with one space between each two (an empty piece still contributes its joining space). Nothing is blanked.
R10 HIT: a DEPENDS-ON directive is a hit when its content contains the seven letters carried in any mix of ASCII case at a
  place whose preceding character is absent or not an ASCII letter and whose following character is absent or not an ASCII
  letter (so carried_x, carried2 and écarried count while miscarried and carriedX do not; a non-ASCII look-alike such as
  U+017F or U+0130 never matches a letter), or when carried_name is not none, not empty and not exactly the text none, and
  the content contains carried_name, compared exactly (case-sensitive, character by character, every character literal) at
  any place, overlapping occurrences included, whose preceding character is absent or not WORD and whose following
  character is absent or not WORD. A name that starts or ends with a non-WORD character still needs those neighbours to be
  non-WORD. CITED FIDELITY package — a name none is never tested (None and NONE are), as it reads like no dependency
R11 Each hit contributes one entry, in ascending line order: line = the directive's line number (R1); what = the WHAT pin;
  text = the directive's line (R1, so with any attribute and trailing comment it holds), cut to its first 96 Unicode code
  points (all of it when shorter). A directive gives at most one entry. No hits gives hits as the empty list.
PINNED charset SP = U+0020 U+0009 CITED FIDELITY package — the parser's one whitespace rule: only space and tab separate, indent and trim; NBSP, U+3000, U+2028, U+FEFF elsewhere are content
PINNED charset WORD = A-Z a-z 0-9 _ CITED FIDELITY package — ASCII only, the R10 name boundaries
PINNED charset LETTER = A-Z a-z CITED FIDELITY package — ASCII only, the R10 carried-word boundaries (digits and _ are not letters)
PINNED charset OPENERS = U+0020 U+0009 ( [ { , = : ; | < > ! & - ~ CITED FIDELITY package — the R8 token starts; not * + ? (regex quantifiers)
PINNED text WHAT = "carried data declared under DEPENDS-ON — use CARRIED (§18)" CITED FIDELITY package — the exact message, an em dash U+2014 and a section sign U+00A7
INVARIANT every hit's line is between 1 and the number of lines (R1) and that line opens a DEPENDS-ON directive; every hit's text
  has at most 96 code points, starts with DEPENDS-ON and has no trailing SP character. The word part of R10 depends on no
  split: it holds exactly when some line of the directive — its own line after the name, a continuation line or a comment
  line, up to the next directive — contains the word; R4, R7 and R8 matter only for a carried_name that holds an SP
  character, a "#" or an attribute word.
OUT-OF-DOMAIN a text or a carried_name that is not text (a number, a list) throws a host type error in the package; a truthy
  non-text name throws even when no DEPENDS-ON directive exists, the number 0 reads as none. A text
  with an unpaired surrogate is OUT (the package reads it by code units and cuts R11 by code points, keeping lone halves).
  None of this is reproduced.
ORACLE value -> oracles/schema_lint.json  CANONICAL-SHA256 0a0916d12c07d545826d2813aaf5d3246311e79169468c5db03e530958430540
SCOPE IN: R1-R11 over ENVELOPE — the parser's directive, continuation, comment, attribute and quoted-span reading only as far
  as DEPENDS-ON content needs it. OUT: OUT-OF-DOMAIN; the parser's errors and warnings (unknown names, glued names, an
  orphan line — all observable only as what R5 and R6 make of the line); strict mode; the pack gate around the lint
  (lintFamily, carriedDeclared, the validator's E_CARRIED_AS_DEPENDENCY mapping).
PROVENANCE rederive@6199e2cc8b75 (pinned selfhost authority), cli/sir/v05.mjs: export function schemaLint(text, carriedName),
  called from lintFamily with carriedDeclared(primary)?.name; it reads parse(text).ast (args, cont, attrList, comments) and
  sirLines(text) from cli/sir/sir-parse.mjs and cut96 from v05.mjs. Round-5b delta re-cut 2026-10-27 (FINDINGS #109: the
  over-read, the carried word, the name none; the parser's U+FEFF lead and opener set); an independent model of R1-R11
  agreed with it on 460,000 inputs (400 generator seeds and a 300,000-input character fuzz), and the INVARIANT's line form
  of the word test on 60,000 more.
